<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>

<pre>

<b>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</b>

     You may remember the wildly upbeat message of last year=92s=20
report: nothing much was in the works but our experience had been=20
that something big popped up occasionally.  This carefully-
crafted corporate strategy paid off in 1985.  Later sections of=20
this report discuss (a) our purchase of a major position in=20
Capital Cities/ABC, (b) our acquisition of Scott &amp; Fetzer, (c)=20
our entry into a large, extended term participation in the=20
insurance business of Fireman=92s Fund, and (d) our sale of our=20
stock in General Foods.

     Our gain in net worth during the year was $613.6 million, or=20
48.2%. It is fitting that the visit of Halley=92s Comet coincided=20
with this percentage gain: neither will be seen again in my=20
lifetime.  Our gain in per-share book value over the last twenty-
one years (that is, since present management took over) has been=20
from $19.46 to $1643.71, or 23.2% compounded annually, another=20
percentage that will not be repeated.

    Two factors make anything approaching this rate of gain=20
unachievable in the future.  One factor probably transitory - is=20
a stock market that offers very little opportunity compared to=20
the markets that prevailed throughout much of the 1964-1984=20
period.  Today we cannot find significantly-undervalued equities=20
to purchase for our insurance company portfolios.  The current=20
situation is 180 degrees removed from that existing about a=20
decade ago, when the only question was which bargain to choose.

     This change in the market also has negative implications for=20
our present portfolio.  In our 1974 annual report I could say: =20
=93We consider several of our major holdings to have great=20
potential for significantly increased values in future years.=94 I=20
can=92t say that now.  It=92s true that our insurance companies=20
currently hold major positions in companies with exceptional=20
underlying economics and outstanding managements, just as they=20
did in 1974.  But current market prices generously appraise these=20
attributes, whereas they were ignored in 1974.  Today=92s=20
valuations mean that our insurance companies have no chance for=20
future portfolio gains on the scale of those achieved in the=20
past.

     The second negative factor, far more telling, is our size. =20
Our equity capital is more than twenty times what it was only ten=20
years ago.  And an iron law of business is that growth eventually=20
dampens exceptional economics. just look at the records of high-
return companies once they have amassed even $1 billion of equity=20
capital.  None that I know of has managed subsequently, over a=20
ten-year period, to keep on earning 20% or more on equity while=20
reinvesting all or substantially all of its earnings.  Instead,=20
to sustain their high returns, such companies have needed to shed=20
a lot of capital by way of either dividends or repurchases of=20
stock.  Their shareholders would have been far better off if all=20
earnings could have been reinvested at the fat returns earned by=20
these exceptional businesses.  But the companies simply couldn=92t=20
turn up enough high-return opportunities to make that possible.

     Their problem is our problem.  Last year I told you that we=20
needed profits of $3.9 billion over the ten years then coming up=20
to earn 15% annually.  The comparable figure for the ten years=20
now ahead is $5.7 billion, a 48% increase that corresponds - as=20
it must mathematically - to the growth in our capital base during=20
1985. (Here=92s a little perspective: leaving aside oil companies,=20
only about 15 U.S. businesses have managed to earn over $5.7=20
billion during the past ten years.)

     Charlie Munger, my partner in managing Berkshire, and I are=20
reasonably optimistic about Berkshire=92s ability to earn returns=20
superior to those earned by corporate America generally, and you=20
will benefit from the company=92s retention of all earnings as long=20
as those returns are forthcoming.  We have several things going=20
for us: (1) we don=92t have to worry about quarterly or annual=20
figures but, instead, can focus on whatever actions will maximize=20
long-term value; (2) we can expand the business into any areas=20
that make sense - our scope is not circumscribed by history,=20
structure, or concept; and (3) we love our work.  All of these=20
help.  Even so, we will also need a full measure of good fortune=20
to average our hoped-for 15% - far more good fortune than was=20
required for our past 23.2%.

     We need to mention one further item in the investment=20
equation that could affect recent purchasers of our stock. =20
Historically, Berkshire shares have sold modestly below intrinsic=20
business value.  With the price there, purchasers could be=20
certain (as long as they did not experience a widening of this=20
discount) that their personal investment experience would at=20
least equal the financial experience of the business.  But=20
recently the discount has disappeared, and occasionally a modest=20
premium has prevailed.

     The elimination of the discount means that Berkshire=92s=20
market value increased even faster than business value (which,=20
itself, grew at a pleasing pace).  That was good news for any=20
owner holding while that move took place, but it is bad news for=20
the new or prospective owner.  If the financial experience of new=20
owners of Berkshire is merely to match the future financial=20
experience of the company, any premium of market value over=20
intrinsic business value that they pay must be maintained.

     Management cannot determine market prices, although it can,=20
by its disclosures and policies, encourage rational behavior by=20
market participants.  My own preference, as perhaps you=92d guess,=20
is for a market price that consistently approximates business=20
value.  Given that relationship, all owners prosper precisely as=20
the business prospers during their period of ownership.  Wild=20
swings in market prices far above and below business value do not=20
change the final gains for owners in aggregate; in the end,=20
investor gains must equal business gains.  But long periods of=20
substantial undervaluation and/or overvaluation will cause the=20
gains of the business to be inequitably distributed among various=20
owners, with the investment result of any given owner largely=20
depending upon how lucky, shrewd, or foolish he happens to be.

     Over the long term there has been a more consistent=20
relationship between Berkshire=92s market value and business value=20
than has existed for any other publicly-traded equity with which=20
I am familiar.  This is a tribute to you.  Because you have been=20
rational, interested, and investment-oriented, the market price=20
for Berkshire stock has almost always been sensible.  This=20
unusual result has been achieved by a shareholder group with=20
unusual demographics: virtually all of our shareholders are=20
individuals, not institutions.  No other public company our size=20
can claim the same.

     You might think that institutions, with their large staffs=20
of highly-paid and experienced investment professionals, would be=20
a force for stability and reason in financial markets.  They are=20
not: stocks heavily owned and constantly monitored by=20
institutions have often been among the most inappropriately=20
valued.

     Ben Graham told a story 40 years ago that illustrates why=20
investment professionals behave as they do: An oil prospector,=20
moving to his heavenly reward, was met by St. Peter with bad=20
news.  =93You=92re qualified for residence=94, said St. Peter, =93but, as=
=20
you can see, the compound reserved for oil men is packed. =20
There=92s no way to squeeze you in.=94 After thinking a moment, the=20
prospector asked if he might say just four words to the present=20
occupants.  That seemed harmless to St. Peter, so the prospector=20
cupped his hands and yelled, =93Oil discovered in hell.=94=20
Immediately the gate to the compound opened and all of the oil=20
men marched out to head for the nether regions.  Impressed, St.=20
Peter invited the prospector to move in and make himself=20
comfortable.  The prospector paused.  =93No,=94 he said, =93I think=20
I=92ll go along with the rest of the boys.  There might be some=20
truth to that rumor after all.=94

<b>Sources of Reported Earnings</b>

     The table on the next page shows the major sources of=20
Berkshire=92s reported earnings.  These numbers, along with far=20
more detailed sub-segment numbers, are the ones that Charlie and=20
I focus upon.  We do not find consolidated figures an aid in=20
either managing or evaluating Berkshire and, in fact, never=20
prepare them for internal use.

     Segment information is equally essential for investors=20
wanting to know what is going on in a multi-line business. =20
Corporate managers always have insisted upon such information=20
before making acquisition decisions but, until a few years ago,=20
seldom made it available to investors faced with acquisition and=20
disposition decisions of their own.  Instead, when owners wishing=20
to understand the economic realities of their business asked for=20
data, managers usually gave them a we-can=92t-tell-you-what-is-
going-on-because-it-would-hurt-the-company answer.  Ultimately=20
the SEC ordered disclosure of segment data and management began=20
supplying real answers.  The change in their behavior recalls an=20
insight of Al Capone: =93You can get much further with a kind word=20
and a gun than you can with a kind word alone.=94

In the table, amortization of Goodwill is not charged against the=20
specific businesses but, for reasons outlined in the Appendix to=20
my letter in the 1983 annual report, is aggregated as a separate=20
item. (A compendium of the 1977-1984 letters is available upon=20
request.) In the Business Segment Data and Management=92s=20
Discussion sections on pages 39-41 and 49-55, much additional=20
information regarding our businesses is provided, including=20
Goodwill and Goodwill Amortization figures for each of the=20
segments.  I urge you to read those sections as well as Charlie=20
Munger=92s letter to Wesco shareholders, which starts on page 56.
