     Our 1985 results include unusually large earnings from the=20
sale of securities.  This fact, in itself, does not mean that we=20
had a particularly good year (though, of course, we did). =20
Security profits in a given year bear similarities to a college=20
graduation ceremony in which the knowledge gained over four years=20
is recognized on a day when nothing further is learned.  We may=20
hold a stock for a decade or more, and during that period it may=20
grow quite consistently in both business and market value.  In=20
the year in which we finally sell it there may be no increase in=20
value, or there may even be a decrease.  But all growth in value=20
since purchase will be reflected in the accounting earnings of=20
the year of sale. (If the stock owned is in our insurance=20
subsidiaries, however, any gain or loss in market value will be=20
reflected in net worth annually.) Thus, reported capital gains or=20
losses in any given year are meaningless as a measure of how well=20
we have done in the current year.

     A large portion of the realized gain in 1985 ($338 million=20
pre-tax out of a total of $488 million) came about through the=20
sale of our General Foods shares.  We held most of these shares=20
since 1980, when we had purchased them at a price far below what=20
we felt was their per/share business value.  Year by year, the=20
managerial efforts of Jim Ferguson and Phil Smith substantially=20
increased General Foods=92 business value and, last fall, Philip=20
Morris made an offer for the company that reflected the increase. =20
We thus benefited from four factors: a bargain purchase price, a=20
business with fine underlying economics, an able management=20
concentrating on the interests of shareholders, and a buyer=20
willing to pay full business value.  While that last factor is=20
the only one that produces reported earnings, we consider=20
identification of the first three to be the key to building value=20
for Berkshire shareholders.  In selecting common stocks, we=20
devote our attention to attractive purchases, not to the=20
possibility of attractive sales.

     We have again reported substantial income from special=20
distributions, this year from Washington Post and General Foods.=20
(The General Foods transactions obviously took place well before=20
the Philip Morris offer.) Distributions of this kind occur when=20
we sell a portion of our shares in a company back to it=20
simultaneously with its purchase of shares from other=20
shareholders.  The number of shares we sell is contractually set=20
so as to leave our percentage ownership in the company precisely=20
the same after the sale as before.  Such a transaction is quite=20
properly regarded by the IRS as substantially equivalent to a=20
dividend since we, as a shareholder, receive cash while=20
maintaining an unchanged ownership interest.  This tax treatment=20
benefits us because corporate taxpayers, unlike individual=20
taxpayers, incur much lower taxes on dividend income than on=20
income from long-term capital gains. (This difference will be=20
widened further if the House-passed tax bill becomes law: under=20
its provisions, capital gains realized by corporations will be=20
taxed at the same rate as ordinary income.) However, accounting=20
rules are unclear as to proper treatment for shareholder=20
reporting.  To conform with last year=92s treatment, we have shown=20
these transactions as capital gains.

     Though we have not sought out such transactions, we have=20
agreed to them on several occasions when managements initiated=20
the idea.  In each case we have felt that non-selling=20
shareholders (all of whom had an opportunity to sell at the same=20
price we received) benefited because the companies made their=20
repurchases at prices below intrinsic business value.  The tax=20
advantages we receive and our wish to cooperate with managements=20
that are increasing values for all shareholders have sometimes=20
led us to sell - but only to the extent that our proportional=20
share of the business was undiminished.

     At this point we usually turn to a discussion of some of our=20
major business units.  Before doing so, however, we should first=20
look at a failure at one of our smaller businesses.  Our Vice=20
Chairman, Charlie Munger, has always emphasized the study of=20
mistakes rather than successes, both in business and other=20
aspects of life.  He does so in the spirit of the man who said:=20
=93All I want to know is where I=92m going to die so I=92ll never go=20
there.=94 You=92ll immediately see why we make a good team: Charlie=20
likes to study errors and I have generated ample material for=20
him, particularly in our textile and insurance businesses.

<b>Shutdown of Textile Business</b>

     In July we decided to close our textile operation, and by=20
yearend this unpleasant job was largely completed.  The history=20
of this business is instructive.

     When Buffett Partnership, Ltd., an investment partnership of=20
which I was general partner, bought control of Berkshire Hathaway=20
21 years ago, it had an accounting net worth of $22 million, all=20
devoted to the textile business.  The company=92s intrinsic=20
business value, however, was considerably less because the=20
textile assets were unable to earn returns commensurate with=20
their accounting value.  Indeed, during the previous nine years=20
(the period in which Berkshire and Hathaway operated as a merged=20
company) aggregate sales of $530 million had produced an=20
aggregate loss of $10 million.  Profits had been reported from=20
time to time but the net effect was always one step forward, two=20
steps back.

     At the time we made our purchase, southern textile plants -=20
largely non-union - were believed to have an important=20
competitive advantage.  Most northern textile operations had=20
closed and many people thought we would liquidate our business as=20
well.

     We felt, however, that the business would be run much better=20
by a long-time employee whom. we immediately selected to be=20
president, Ken Chace.  In this respect we were 100% correct: Ken=20
and his recent successor, Garry Morrison, have been excellent=20
managers, every bit the equal of managers at our more profitable=20
businesses.

     In early 1967 cash generated by the textile operation was=20
used to fund our entry into insurance via the purchase of=20
National Indemnity Company.  Some of the money came from earnings=20
and some from reduced investment in textile inventories,=20
receivables, and fixed assets.  This pullback proved wise:=20
although much improved by Ken=92s management, the textile business=20
never became a good earner, not even in cyclical upturns.

     Further diversification for Berkshire followed, and=20
gradually the textile operation=92s depressing effect on our=20
overall return diminished as the business became a progressively=20
smaller portion of the corporation.  We remained in the business=20
for reasons that I stated in the 1978 annual report (and=20
summarized at other times also): =93(1) our textile businesses are=20
very important employers in their communities, (2) management has=20
been straightforward in reporting on problems and energetic in=20
attacking them, (3) labor has been cooperative and understanding=20
in facing our common problems, and (4) the business should=20
average modest cash returns relative to investment.=94 I further=20
said, =93As long as these conditions prevail - and we expect that=20
they will - we intend to continue to support our textile business=20
despite more attractive alternative uses for capital.=94

     It turned out that I was very wrong about (4).  Though 1979=20
was moderately profitable, the business thereafter consumed major=20
amounts of cash. By mid-1985 it became clear, even to me, that=20
this condition was almost sure to continue.  Could we have found=20
a buyer who would continue operations, I would have certainly=20
preferred to sell the business rather than liquidate it, even if=20
that meant somewhat lower proceeds for us.  But the economics=20
that were finally obvious to me were also obvious to others, and=20
interest was nil.

     I won=92t close down businesses of sub-normal profitability=20
merely to add a fraction of a point to our corporate rate of=20
return.  However, I also feel it inappropriate for even an=20
exceptionally profitable company to fund an operation once it=20
appears to have unending losses in prospect.  Adam Smith would=20
disagree with my first proposition, and Karl Marx would disagree=20
with my second; the middle ground is the only position that=20
leaves me comfortable.

     I should reemphasize that Ken and Garry have been=20
resourceful, energetic and imaginative in attempting to make our=20
textile operation a success.  Trying to achieve sustainable=20
profitability, they reworked product lines, machinery=20
configurations and distribution arrangements.  We also made a=20
major acquisition, Waumbec Mills, with the expectation of=20
important synergy (a term widely used in business to explain an=20
acquisition that otherwise makes no sense).  But in the end=20
nothing worked and I should be faulted for not quitting sooner. =20
A recent <i>Business Week</i> article stated that 250 textile mills have=20
closed since 1980.  Their owners were not privy to any=20
information that was unknown to me; they simply processed it more=20
objectively.  I ignored Comte=92s advice - =93the intellect should be=20
the servant of the heart, but not its slave=94 - and believed what=20
I preferred to believe.

     The domestic textile industry operates in a commodity=20
business, competing in a world market in which substantial excess=20
capacity exists.  Much of the trouble we experienced was=20
attributable, both directly and indirectly, to competition from=20
foreign countries whose workers are paid a small fraction of the=20
U.S. minimum wage.  But that in no way means that our labor force=20
deserves any blame for our closing.  In fact, in comparison with=20
employees of American industry generally, our workers were poorly=20
paid, as has been the case throughout the textile business.  In=20
contract negotiations, union leaders and members were sensitive=20
to our disadvantageous cost position and did not push for=20
unrealistic wage increases or unproductive work practices.  To=20
the contrary, they tried just as hard as we did to keep us=20
competitive.  Even during our liquidation period they performed=20
superbly. (Ironically, we would have been better off financially=20
if our union had behaved unreasonably some years ago; we then=20
would have recognized the impossible future that we faced,=20
promptly closed down, and avoided significant future losses.)

     Over the years, we had the option of making large capital=20
expenditures in the textile operation that would have allowed us=20
to somewhat reduce variable costs.  Each proposal to do so looked=20
like an immediate winner.  Measured by standard return-on-
investment tests, in fact, these proposals usually promised=20
greater economic benefits than would have resulted from=20
comparable expenditures in our highly-profitable candy and=20
newspaper businesses.

     But the promised benefits from these textile investments=20
were illusory.  Many of our competitors, both domestic and=20
foreign, were stepping up to the same kind of expenditures and,=20
once enough companies did so, their reduced costs became the=20
baseline for reduced prices industrywide.  Viewed individually,=20
each company=92s capital investment decision appeared cost-
effective and rational; viewed collectively, the decisions=20
neutralized each other and were irrational (just as happens when=20
each person watching a parade decides he can see a little better=20
if he stands on tiptoes).  After each round of investment, all=20
the players had more money in the game and returns remained=20
anemic.

     Thus, we faced a miserable choice: huge capital investment=20
would have helped to keep our textile business alive, but would=20
have left us with terrible returns on ever-growing amounts of=20
capital.  After the investment, moreover, the foreign competition=20
would still have retained a major, continuing advantage in labor=20
costs.  A refusal to invest, however, would make us increasingly=20
non-competitive, even measured against domestic textile=20
manufacturers.  I always thought myself in the position described=20
by Woody Allen in one of his movies: =93More than any other time in=20
history, mankind faces a crossroads.  One path leads to despair=20
and utter hopelessness, the other to total extinction.  Let us=20
pray we have the wisdom to choose correctly.=94

     For an understanding of how the to-invest-or-not-to-invest=20
dilemma plays out in a commodity business, it is instructive to=20
look at Burlington Industries, by far the largest U.S. textile=20
company both 21 years ago and now.  In 1964 Burlington had sales=20
of $1.2 billion against our $50 million.  It had strengths in=20
both distribution and production that we could never hope to=20
match and also, of course, had an earnings record far superior to=20
ours.  Its stock sold at 60 at the end of 1964; ours was 13.

     Burlington made a decision to stick to the textile business,=20
and in 1985 had sales of about $2.8 billion.  During the 1964-85=20
period, the company made capital expenditures of about $3=20
billion, far more than any other U.S. textile company and more=20
than $200-per-share on that $60 stock.  A very large part of the=20
expenditures, I am sure, was devoted to cost improvement and=20
expansion.  Given Burlington=92s basic commitment to stay in=20
textiles, I would also surmise that the company=92s capital=20
decisions were quite rational.

     Nevertheless, Burlington has lost sales volume in real=20
dollars and has far lower returns on sales and equity now than 20=20
years ago.  Split 2-for-1 in 1965, the stock now sells at 34 --=20
on an adjusted basis, just a little over its $60 price in 1964. =20
Meanwhile, the CPI has more than tripled.  Therefore, each share=20
commands about one-third the purchasing power it did at the end=20
of 1964.  Regular dividends have been paid but they, too, have=20
shrunk significantly in purchasing power.

     This devastating outcome for the shareholders indicates what=20
can happen when much brain power and energy are applied to a=20
faulty premise.  The situation is suggestive of Samuel Johnson=92s=20
horse: =93A horse that can count to ten is a remarkable horse - not=20
a remarkable mathematician.=94 Likewise, a textile company that=20
allocates capital brilliantly within its industry is a remarkable=20
textile company - but not a remarkable business.

     My conclusion from my own experiences and from much=20
observation of other businesses is that a good managerial record=20
(measured by economic returns) is far more a function of what=20
business boat you get into than it is of how effectively you row=20
(though intelligence and effort help considerably, of course, in=20
any business, good or bad).  Some years ago I wrote: =93When a=20
management with a reputation for brilliance tackles a business=20
with a reputation for poor fundamental economics, it is the=20
reputation of the business that remains intact.=94 Nothing has=20
since changed my point of view on that matter.  Should you find=20
yourself in a chronically-leaking boat, energy devoted to=20
changing vessels is likely to be more productive than energy=20
devoted to patching leaks.

                            *  *  *

     There is an investment postscript in our textile saga.  Some=20
investors weight book value heavily in their stock-buying=20
decisions (as I, in my early years, did myself).  And some=20
economists and academicians believe replacement values are of=20
considerable importance in calculating an appropriate price level=20
for the stock market as a whole.  Those of both persuasions would=20
have received an education at the auction we held in early 1986=20
to dispose of our textile machinery.

     The equipment sold (including some disposed of in the few=20
months prior to the auction) took up about 750,000 square feet of=20
factory space in New Bedford and was eminently usable.  It=20
originally cost us about $13 million, including $2 million spent=20
in 1980-84, and had a current book value of $866,000 (after=20
accelerated depreciation).  Though no sane management would have=20
made the investment, the equipment could have been replaced new=20
for perhaps $30-$50 million.

     Gross proceeds from our sale of this equipment came to=20
$163,122.  Allowing for necessary pre- and post-sale costs, our=20
net was less than zero.  Relatively modern looms that we bought=20
for $5,000 apiece in 1981 found no takers at $50.  We finally=20
sold them for scrap at $26 each, a sum less than removal costs.

     Ponder this: the economic goodwill attributable to two paper=20
routes in Buffalo - or a single See=92s candy store - considerably=20
exceeds the proceeds we received from this massive collection of=20
tangible assets that not too many years ago, under different=20
competitive conditions, was able to employ over 1,000 people.

<b>Three Very Good Businesses (and a Few Thoughts About Incentive=20
Compensation)</b>

     When I was 12, I lived with my grandfather for about four=20
months.  A grocer by trade, he was also working on a book and=20
each night he dictated a few pages to me.  The title - brace=20
yourself - was =93How to Run a Grocery Store and a Few Things I=20
Have Learned About Fishing=94.  My grandfather was sure that=20
interest in these two subjects was universal and that the world=20
awaited his views.  You may conclude from this section=92s title=20
and contents that I was overexposed to Grandpa=92s literary style=20
(and personality).

     I am merging the discussion of Nebraska Furniture Mart,=20
See=92s Candy Shops, and Buffalo Evening News here because the=20
economic strengths, weaknesses, and prospects of these businesses=20
have changed little since I reported to you a year ago.  The=20
shortness of this discussion, however, is in no way meant to=20
minimize the importance of these businesses to us: in 1985 they=20
earned an aggregate of $72 million pre-tax.  Fifteen years ago,=20
before we had acquired any of them, their aggregate earnings were=20
about $8 million pre-tax.

     While an increase in earnings from $8 million to $72 million=20
sounds terrific - and usually is - you should not automatically=20
assume that to be the case.  You must first make sure that=20
earnings were not severely depressed in the base year.  If they=20
were instead substantial in relation to capital employed, an even=20
more important point must be examined: how much additional=20
capital was required to produce the additional earnings?

     In both respects, our group of three scores well.  First,=20
earnings 15 years ago were excellent compared to capital then=20
employed in the businesses.  Second, although annual earnings are=20
now $64 million greater, the businesses require only about $40=20
million more in invested capital to operate than was the case=20
then.

     The dramatic growth in earning power of these three=20
businesses, accompanied by their need for only minor amounts of=20
capital, illustrates very well the power of economic goodwill=20
during an inflationary period (a phenomenon explained in detail=20
in the 1983 annual report).  The financial characteristics of=20
these businesses have allowed us to use a very large portion of=20
the earnings they generate elsewhere.  Corporate America,=20
however, has had a different experience: in order to increase=20
earnings significantly, most companies have needed to increase=20
capital significantly also.  The average American business has=20
required about $5 of additional capital to generate an additional=20
$1 of annual pre-tax earnings.  That business, therefore, would=20
have required over $300 million in additional capital from its=20
owners in order to achieve an earnings performance equal to our=20
group of three.

     When returns on capital are ordinary, an earn-more-by-
putting-up-more record is no great managerial achievement.  You=20
can get the same result personally while operating from your=20
rocking chair. just quadruple the capital you commit to a savings=20
account and you will quadruple your earnings.  You would hardly=20
expect hosannas for that particular accomplishment.  Yet,=20
retirement announcements regularly sing the praises of CEOs who=20
have, say, quadrupled earnings of their widget company during=20
their reign - with no one examining whether this gain was=20
attributable simply to many years of retained earnings and the=20
workings of compound interest.

     If the widget company consistently earned a superior return=20
on capital throughout the period, or if capital employed only=20
doubled during the CEO=92s reign, the praise for him may be well=20
deserved.  But if return on capital was lackluster and capital=20
employed increased in pace with earnings, applause should be=20
withheld.  A savings account in which interest was reinvested=20
would achieve the same year-by-year increase in earnings - and,=20
at only 8% interest, would quadruple its annual earnings in 18=20
years.

     The power of this simple math is often ignored by companies=20
to the detriment of their shareholders.  Many corporate=20
compensation plans reward managers handsomely for earnings=20
increases produced solely, or in large part, by retained earnings=20
- i.e., earnings withheld from owners.  For example, ten-year,=20
fixed-price stock options are granted routinely, often by=20
companies whose dividends are only a small percentage of=20
earnings.

     An example will illustrate the inequities possible under=20
such circumstances.  Let=92s suppose that you had a $100,000=20
savings account earning 8% interest and =93managed=94 by a trustee=20
who could decide each year what portion of the interest you were=20
to be paid in cash.  Interest not paid out would be =93retained=20
earnings=94 added to the savings account to compound.  And let=92s=20
suppose that your trustee, in his superior wisdom, set the =93pay-
out ratio=94 at one-quarter of the annual earnings.

     Under these assumptions, your account would be worth=20
$179,084 at the end of ten years.  Additionally, your annual=20
earnings would have increased about 70% from $8,000 to $13,515=20
under this inspired management.  And, finally, your =93dividends=94=20
would have increased commensurately, rising regularly from $2,000=20
in the first year to $3,378 in the tenth year.  Each year, when=20
your manager=92s public relations firm prepared his annual report=20
to you, all of the charts would have had lines marching skyward.

     Now, just for fun, let=92s push our scenario one notch further=20
and give your trustee-manager a ten-year fixed-price option on=20
part of your =93business=94 (i.e., your savings account) based on its=20
fair value in the first year.  With such an option, your manager=20
would reap a substantial profit at your expense - just from=20
having held on to most of your earnings.  If he were both=20
Machiavellian and a bit of a mathematician, your manager might=20
also have cut the pay-out ratio once he was firmly entrenched.

     This scenario is not as farfetched as you might think.  Many=20
stock options in the corporate world have worked in exactly that=20
fashion: they have gained in value simply because management=20
retained earnings, not because it did well with the capital in=20
its hands.

     Managers actually apply a double standard to options. =20
Leaving aside warrants (which deliver the issuing corporation=20
immediate and substantial compensation), I believe it is fair to=20
say that nowhere in the business world are ten-year fixed-price=20
options on all or a portion of a business granted to outsiders. =20
Ten months, in fact, would be regarded as extreme.  It would be=20
particularly unthinkable for managers to grant a long-term option=20
on a business that was regularly adding to its capital.  Any=20
outsider wanting to secure such an option would be required to=20
pay fully for capital added during the option period.

     The unwillingness of managers to do-unto-outsiders, however,=20
is not matched by an unwillingness to do-unto-themselves.=20
(Negotiating with one=92s self seldom produces a barroom brawl.)=20
Managers regularly engineer ten-year, fixed-price options for=20
themselves and associates that, first, totally ignore the fact=20
that retained earnings automatically build value and, second,=20
ignore the carrying cost of capital.  As a result, these managers=20
end up profiting much as they would have had they had an option=20
on that savings account that was automatically building up in=20
value.

     Of course, stock options often go to talented, value-adding=20
managers and sometimes deliver them rewards that are perfectly=20
appropriate. (Indeed, managers who are really exceptional almost=20
always get far less than they should.) But when the result is=20
equitable, it is accidental.  Once granted, the option is blind=20
to individual performance.  Because it is irrevocable and=20
unconditional (so long as a manager stays in the company), the=20
sluggard receives rewards from his options precisely as does the=20
star.  A managerial Rip Van Winkle, ready to doze for ten years,=20
could not wish for a better =93incentive=94 system.

     (I can=92t resist commenting on one long-term option given an=20
=93outsider=94: that granted the U.S. Government on Chrysler shares=20
as partial consideration for the government=92s guarantee of some=20
lifesaving loans.  When these options worked out well for the=20
government, Chrysler sought to modify the payoff, arguing that=20
the rewards to the government were both far greater than intended=20
and outsize in relation to its contribution to Chrysler=92s=20
recovery.  The company=92s anguish over what it saw as an imbalance=20
between payoff and performance made national news.  That anguish=20
may well be unique: to my knowledge, no managers - anywhere -=20
have been similarly offended by unwarranted payoffs arising from=20
options granted to themselves or their colleagues.)

     Ironically, the rhetoric about options frequently describes=20
them as desirable because they put managers and owners in the=20
same financial boat.  In reality, the boats are far different. =20
No owner has ever escaped the burden of capital costs, whereas a=20
holder of a fixed-price option bears no capital costs at all.  An=20
owner must weigh upside potential against downside risk; an=20
option holder has no downside.  In fact, the business project in=20
which you would wish to have an option frequently is a project in=20
which you would reject ownership. (I=92ll be happy to accept a=20
lottery ticket as a gift - but I=92ll never buy one.)

     In dividend policy also, the option holders=92 interests are=20
best served by a policy that may ill serve the owner.  Think back=20
to the savings account example.  The trustee, holding his option,=20
would benefit from a no-dividend policy.  Conversely, the owner=20
of the account should lean to a total payout so that he can=20
prevent the option-holding manager from sharing in the account=92s=20
retained earnings.

     Despite their shortcomings, options can be appropriate under=20
some circumstances.  My criticism relates to their indiscriminate=20
use and, in that connection, I would like to emphasize three=20
points:

     First, stock options are inevitably tied to the overall=20
performance of a corporation.  Logically, therefore, they should=20
be awarded only to those managers with overall responsibility. =20
Managers with limited areas of responsibility should have=20
incentives that pay off in relation to results under their=20
control.  The .350 hitter expects, and also deserves, a big=20
payoff for his performance - even if he plays for a cellar-
dwelling team.  And the .150 hitter should get no reward - even=20
if he plays for a pennant winner.  Only those with overall=20
responsibility for the team should have their rewards tied to its=20
results.

     Second, options should be structured carefully.  Absent=20
special factors, they should have built into them a retained-
earnings or carrying-cost factor.  Equally important, they should=20
be priced realistically.  When managers are faced with offers for=20
their companies, they unfailingly point out how unrealistic=20
market prices can be as an index of real value.  But why, then,=20
should these same depressed prices be the valuations at which=20
managers sell portions of their businesses to themselves? (They=20
may go further: officers and directors sometimes consult the Tax=20
Code to determine the <i>lowest</i> prices at which they can, in effect,=20
sell part of the business to insiders.  While they=92re at it, they=20
often elect plans that produce the worst tax result for the=20
company.) Except in highly unusual cases, owners are not well=20
served by the sale of part of their business at a bargain price -=20
whether the sale is to outsiders or to insiders.  The obvious=20
conclusion: options should be priced at true business value.

     Third, I want to emphasize that some managers whom I admire=20
enormously - and whose operating records are far better than mine=20
- disagree with me regarding fixed-price options.  They have=20
built corporate cultures that work, and fixed-price options have=20
been a tool that helped them.  By their leadership and example,=20
and by the use of options as incentives, these managers have=20
taught their colleagues to think like owners.  Such a Culture is=20
rare and when it exists should perhaps be left intact - despite=20
inefficiencies and inequities that may infest the option program. =20
=93If it ain=92t broke, don=92t fix it=94 is preferable to =93purity at any=
=20
price=94.

     At Berkshire, however, we use an incentive@compensation=20
system that rewards key managers for meeting targets in their own=20
bailiwicks.  If See=92s does well, that does not produce incentive=20
compensation at the News - nor vice versa.  Neither do we look at=20
the price of Berkshire stock when we write bonus checks.  We=20
believe good unit performance should be rewarded whether=20
Berkshire stock rises, falls, or stays even.  Similarly, we think=20
average performance should earn no special rewards even if our=20
stock should soar.  =93Performance=94, furthermore, is defined in=20
different ways depending upon the underlying economics of the=20
business: in some our managers enjoy tailwinds not of their own=20
making, in others they fight unavoidable headwinds.

     The rewards that go with this system can be large.  At our=20
various business units, top managers sometimes receive incentive=20
bonuses of five times their base salary, or more, and it would=20
appear possible that one manager=92s bonus could top $2 million in=20
1986. (I hope so.) We do not put a cap on bonuses, and the=20
potential for rewards is not hierarchical.  The manager of a=20
relatively small unit can earn far more than the manager of a=20
larger unit if results indicate he should.  We believe, further,=20
that such factors as seniority and age should not affect=20
incentive compensation (though they sometimes influence basic=20
compensation).  A 20-year-old who can hit .300 is as valuable to=20
us as a 40-year-old performing as well.

     Obviously, all Berkshire managers can use their bonus money=20
(or other funds, including borrowed money) to buy our stock in=20
the market.  Many have done just that - and some now have large=20
holdings.  By accepting both the risks and the carrying costs=20
that go with outright purchases, these managers truly walk in the=20
shoes of owners.

     Now let=92s get back - at long last - to our three businesses:

     At Nebraska Furniture Mart our basic strength is an=20
exceptionally low-cost operation that allows the business to=20
regularly offer customers the best values available in home=20
furnishings.  NFM is the largest store of its kind in the=20
country.  Although the already-depressed farm economy worsened=20
considerably in 1985, the store easily set a new sales record.  I=20
also am happy to report that NFM=92s Chairman, Rose Blumkin (the=20
legendary =93Mrs.  B=94), continues at age 92 to set a pace at the=20
store that none of us can keep up with.  She=92s there wheeling and=20
dealing seven days a week, and I hope that any of you who visit=20
Omaha will go out to the Mart and see her in action.  It will=20
inspire you, as it does me.

     At See=92s we continue to get store volumes that are far=20
beyond those achieved by any competitor we know of.  Despite the=20
unmatched consumer acceptance we enjoy, industry trends are not=20
good, and we continue to experience slippage in poundage sales on=20
a same-store basis.  This puts pressure on per-pound costs.  We=20
now are willing to increase prices only modestly and, unless we=20
can stabilize per-shop poundage, profit margins will narrow.

     At the News volume gains are also difficult to achieve. =20
Though linage increased during 1985, the gain was more than=20
accounted for by preprints.  ROP linage (advertising printed on=20
our own pages) declined.  Preprints are far less profitable than=20
ROP ads, and also more vulnerable to competition.  In 1985, the=20
News again controlled costs well and our household penetration=20
continues to be exceptional.

     One problem these three operations do not have is=20
management.  At See=92s we have Chuck Huggins, the man we put in=20
charge the day we bought the business.  Selecting him remains one=20
of our best business decisions.  At the News we have Stan Lipsey,=20
a manager of equal caliber.  Stan has been with us 17 years, and=20
his unusual business talents have become more evident with every=20
additional level of responsibility he has tackled.  And, at the=20
Mart, we have the amazing Blumkins - Mrs. B, Louie, Ron, Irv, and=20
Steve - a three-generation miracle of management.

     I consider myself extraordinarily lucky to be able to work=20
with managers such as these.  I like them personally as much as I=20
admire them professionally.

