     The combined ratio represents total insurance costs (losses=20
incurred plus expenses) compared to revenue from premiums: a=20
ratio below 100 indicates an underwriting profit, and one above=20
100 indicates a loss.

     The industry=92s 1985 results were highly unusual.  The=20
revenue gain was exceptional, and had insured losses grown at=20
their normal rate of most recent years - that is, a few points=20
above the inflation rate - a significant drop in the combined=20
ratio would have occurred.  But losses in 1985 didn=92t cooperate,=20
as they did not in 1984.  Though inflation slowed considerably in=20
these years, insured losses perversely accelerated, growing by=20
16% in 1984 and by an even more startling 17% in 1985.  The=20
year=92s growth in losses therefore exceeds the inflation rate by=20
over 13 percentage points, a modern record.

     Catastrophes were not the culprit in this explosion of loss=20
cost.  True, there were an unusual number of hurricanes in 1985,=20
but the aggregate damage caused by all catastrophes in 1984 and=20
1985 was about 2% of premium volume, a not unusual proportion. =20
Nor was there any burst in the number of insured autos, houses,=20
employers, or other kinds of =93exposure units=94.

     A partial explanation for the surge in the loss figures is=20
all the additions to reserves that the industry made in 1985.  As=20
results for the year were reported, the scene resembled a revival=20
meeting: shouting =93I=92ve sinned, I=92ve sinned=94, insurance managers=20
rushed forward to confess they had under reserved in earlier=20
years.  Their corrections significantly affected 1985 loss=20
numbers.

     A more disturbing ingredient in the loss surge is the=20
acceleration in =93social=94 or =93judicial=94 inflation.  The insurer=92s=
=20
ability to pay has assumed overwhelming importance with juries=20
and judges in the assessment of both liability and damages.  More=20
and more, =93the deep pocket=94 is being sought and found, no matter=20
what the policy wording, the facts, or the precedents.

     This judicial inflation represents a wild card in the=20
industry=92s future, and makes forecasting difficult. =20
Nevertheless, the short-term outlook is good.  Premium growth=20
improved as 1985 went along (quarterly gains were an estimated=20
15%, 19%, 24%, and 22%) and, barring a supercatastrophe, the=20
industry=92s combined ratio should fall sharply in 1986.

     The profit improvement, however, is likely to be of short=20
duration.  Two economic principles will see to that.  First,=20
commodity businesses achieve good levels of profitability only=20
when prices are fixed in some manner or when capacity is short. =20
Second, managers quickly add to capacity when prospects start to=20
improve and capital is available.

     In my 1982 report to you, I discussed the commodity nature=20
of the insurance industry extensively.  The typical policyholder=20
does not differentiate between products but concentrates instead=20
on price.  For many decades a cartel-like procedure kept prices=20
up, but this arrangement has disappeared for good.  The insurance=20
product now is priced as any other commodity for which a free=20
market exists: when capacity is tight, prices will be set=20
remuneratively; otherwise, they will not be.

     Capacity currently is tight in many lines of insurance -=20
though in this industry, unlike most, capacity is an attitudinal=20
concept, not a physical fact.  Insurance managers can write=20
whatever amount of business they feel comfortable writing,=20
subject only to pressures applied by regulators and Best=92s, the=20
industry=92s authoritative rating service.  The comfort level of=20
both managers and regulators is tied to capital.  More capital=20
means more comfort, which in turn means more capacity.  In the=20
typical commodity business, furthermore, such as aluminum or=20
steel, a long gestation precedes the birth of additional=20
capacity.  In the insurance industry, capital can be secured=20
instantly.  Thus, any capacity shortage can be eliminated in=20
short order.

     That=92s exactly what=92s going on right now.  In 1985, about 15=20
insurers raised well over $3 billion, piling up capital so that=20
they can write all the business possible at the better prices now=20
available.  The capital-raising trend has accelerated=20
dramatically so far in 1986.

     If capacity additions continue at this rate, it won=92t be=20
long before serious price-cutting appears and next a fall in=20
profitability.  When the fall comes, it will be the fault of the=20
capital-raisers of 1985 and 1986, not the price-cutters of 198X.=20
(Critics should be understanding, however: as was the case in our=20
textile example, the dynamics of capitalism cause each insurer to=20
make decisions that for itself appear sensible, but that=20
collectively slash profitability.)

     In past reports, I have told you that Berkshire=92s strong=20
capital position - the best in the industry - should one day=20
allow us to claim a distinct competitive advantage in the=20
insurance market.  With the tightening of the market, that day=20
arrived.  Our premium volume more than tripled last year,=20
following a long period of stagnation.  Berkshire=92s financial=20
strength (and our record of maintaining unusual strength through=20
thick and thin) is now a major asset for us in securing good=20
business.

     We correctly foresaw a flight to quality by many large=20
buyers of insurance and reinsurance who belatedly recognized that=20
a policy is only an IOU - and who, in 1985, could not collect on=20
many of their IOUs.  These buyers today are attracted to=20
Berkshire because of its strong capital position.  But, in a=20
development we did not foresee, we also are finding buyers drawn=20
to us because our ability to insure substantial risks sets us=20
apart from the crowd.

     To understand this point, you need a few background facts=20
about large risks.  Traditionally, many insurers have wanted to=20
write this kind of business.  However, their willingness to do so=20
has been almost always based upon reinsurance arrangements that=20
allow the insurer to keep just a small portion of the risk itself=20
while passing on (=93laying off=94) most of the risk to its=20
reinsurers.  Imagine, for example, a directors and officers=20
(=93D &amp; O=94) liability policy providing $25 million of coverage. =20
By various =93excess-of-loss=94 reinsurance contracts, the company=20
issuing that policy might keep the liability for only the first=20
$1 million of any loss that occurs.  The liability for any loss=20
above that amount up to $24 million would be borne by the=20
reinsurers of the issuing insurer.  In trade parlance, a company=20
that issues large policies but retains relatively little of the=20
risk for its own account writes a large gross line but a small=20
net line.

     In any reinsurance arrangement, a key question is how the=20
premiums paid for the policy should be divided among the various=20
=93layers=94 of risk.  In our D &amp; O policy, for example. what part of=
=20
the premium received should be kept by the issuing company to=20
compensate it fairly for taking the first $1 million of risk and=20
how much should be passed on to the reinsurers to compensate them=20
fairly for taking the risk between $1 million and $25 million?

     One way to solve this problem might be deemed the Patrick=20
Henry approach: =93I have but one lamp by which my feet are guided,=20
and that is the lamp of experience.=94 In other words, how much of=20
the total premium would reinsurers have needed in the past to=20
compensate them fairly for the losses they actually had to bear?

     Unfortunately, the lamp of experience has always provided=20
imperfect illumination for reinsurers because so much of their=20
business is =93long-tail=94, meaning it takes many years before they=20
know what their losses are.  Lately, however, the light has not=20
only been dim but also grossly misleading in the images it has=20
revealed.  That is, the courts=92 tendency to grant awards that are=20
both huge and lacking in precedent makes reinsurers=92 usual=20
extrapolations or inferences from past data a formula for=20
disaster.  Out with Patrick Henry and in with Pogo: =93The future=20
ain=92t what it used to be.=94

     The burgeoning uncertainties of the business, coupled with=20
the entry into reinsurance of many unsophisticated participants,=20
worked in recent years in favor of issuing companies writing a=20
small net line: they were able to keep a far greater percentage=20
of the premiums than the risk.  By doing so, the issuing=20
companies sometimes made money on business that was distinctly=20
unprofitable for the issuing and reinsuring companies combined.=20
(This result was not necessarily by intent: issuing companies=20
generally knew no more than reinsurers did about the ultimate=20
costs that would be experienced at higher layers of risk.)=20
Inequities of this sort have been particularly pronounced in=20
lines of insurance in which much change was occurring and losses=20
were soaring; e.g., professional malpractice, D &amp; 0, products=20
liability, etc.  Given these circumstances, it is not surprising=20
that issuing companies remained enthusiastic about writing=20
business long after premiums became woefully inadequate on a=20
gross basis.

     An example of just how disparate results have been for=20
issuing companies versus their reinsurers is provided by the 1984=20
financials of one of the leaders in large and unusual risks.  In=20
that year the company wrote about $6 billion of business and kept=20
around $2 1/2 billion of the premiums, or about 40%.  It gave the=20
remaining $3 1/2 billion to reinsurers.  On the part of the=20
business kept, the company=92s underwriting loss was less than $200=20
million - an excellent result in that year.  Meanwhile, the part=20
laid off produced a loss of over $1.5 billion for the reinsurers. =20
Thus, the issuing company wrote at a combined ratio of well under=20
110 while its reinsurers, participating in precisely the same=20
policies, came in considerably over 140.  This result was not=20
attributable to natural catastrophes; it came from run-of-the-
mill insurance losses (occurring, however, in surprising=20
frequency and size).  The issuing company=92s 1985 report is not=20
yet available, but I would predict it will show that dramatically=20
unbalanced results continued.

     A few years such as this, and even slow-witted reinsurers=20
can lose interest, particularly in explosive lines where the=20
proper split in premium between issuer and reinsurer remains=20
impossible to even roughly estimate.  The behavior of reinsurers=20
finally becomes like that of Mark Twain=92s cat: having once sat on=20
a hot stove, it never did so again - but it never again sat on a=20
cold stove, either.  Reinsurers have had so many unpleasant=20
surprises in long-tail casualty lines that many have decided=20
(probably correctly) to give up the game entirely, regardless of=20
price inducements.  Consequently, there has been a dramatic pull-
back of reinsurance capacity in certain important lines.

     This development has left many issuing companies under=20
pressure.  They can no longer commit their reinsurers, time after=20
time, for tens of millions per policy as they so easily could do=20
only a year or two ago, and they do not have the capital and/or=20
appetite to take on large risks for their own account.  For many=20
issuing companies, gross capacity has shrunk much closer to net=20
capacity - and that is often small, indeed.

     At Berkshire we have never played the lay-it-off-at-a-profit=20
game and, until recently, that put us at a severe disadvantage in=20
certain lines.  Now the tables are turned: we have the=20
underwriting capability whereas others do not.  If we believe the=20
price to be right, we are willing to write a net line larger than=20
that of any but the largest insurers.  For instance, we are=20
perfectly willing to risk losing $10 million of our own money on=20
a single event, as long as we believe that the price is right and=20
that the risk of loss is not significantly correlated with other=20
risks we are insuring.  Very few insurers are willing to risk=20
half that much on single events - although, just a short while=20
ago, many were willing to lose five or ten times that amount as=20
long as virtually all of the loss was for the account of their=20
reinsurers.

     In mid-1985 our largest insurance company, National=20
Indemnity Company, broadcast its willingness to underwrite large=20
risks by running an ad in three issues of an insurance weekly. =20
The ad solicited policies of only large size: those with a=20
minimum premium of $1 million.  This ad drew a remarkable 600=20
replies and ultimately produced premiums totaling about $50=20
million. (Hold the applause: it=92s all long-tail business and it=20
will be at least five years before we know whether this marketing=20
success was also an underwriting success.) Today, our insurance=20
subsidiaries continue to be sought out by brokers searching for=20
large net capacity.

     As I have said, this period of tightness will pass; insurers=20
and reinsurers will return to underpricing.  But for a year or=20
two we should do well in several segments of our insurance=20
business.  Mike Goldberg has made many important improvements in=20
the operation (prior mismanagement by your Chairman having=20
provided him ample opportunity to do so).  He has been=20
particularly successful recently in hiring young managers with=20
excellent potential.  They will have a chance to show their stuff=20
in 1986.

     Our combined ratio has improved - from 134 in 1984 to 111 in=20
1985 - but continues to reflect past misdeeds.  Last year I told=20
you of the major mistakes I had made in loss-reserving, and=20
promised I would update you annually on loss-development figures. =20
Naturally, I made this promise thinking my future record would be=20
much improved.  So far this has not been the case.  Details on=20
last year=92s loss development are on pages 50-52.  They reveal=20
significant underreserving at the end of 1984, as they did in the=20
several years preceding.

     The only bright spot in this picture is that virtually all=20
of the underreserving revealed in 1984 occurred in the=20
reinsurance area - and there, in very large part, in a few=20
contracts that were discontinued several years ago.  This=20
explanation, however, recalls all too well a story told me many=20
years ago by the then Chairman of General Reinsurance Company. =20
He said that every year his managers told him that =93except for=20
the Florida hurricane=94 or =93except for Midwestern tornadoes=94, they=20
would have had a terrific year.  Finally he called the group=20
together and suggested that they form a new operation - the=20
Except-For Insurance Company - in which they would henceforth=20
place all of the business that they later wouldn=92t want to count.

     In any business, insurance or otherwise, =93except for=94 should=20
be excised from the lexicon.  If you are going to play the game,=20
you must count the runs scored against you in all nine innings. =20
Any manager who consistently says =93except for=94 and then reports=20
on the lessons he has learned from his mistakes may be missing=20
the only important lesson - namely, that the real mistake is not=20
the act, but the actor.

     Inevitably, of course, business errors will occur and the=20
wise manager will try to find the proper lessons in them.  But=20
the trick is to learn most lessons from the experiences of=20
others.  Managers who have learned much from personal experience=20
in the past usually are destined to learn much from personal=20
experience in the future.

     GEICO, 38%-owned by Berkshire, reported an excellent year in=20
