     We mentioned earlier that in the past decade the investment=20
environment has changed from one in which great businesses were=20
totally unappreciated to one in which they are appropriately=20
recognized.  The Washington Post Company (=93WPC=94) provides an=20
excellent example.

     We bought all of our WPC holdings in mid-1973 at a price of=20
not more than one-fourth of the then per-share business value of=20
the enterprise.  Calculating the price/value ratio required no=20
unusual insights.  Most security analysts, media brokers, and=20
media executives would have estimated WPC=92s intrinsic business=20
value at $400 to $500 million just as we did.  And its $100=20
million stock market valuation was published daily for all to=20
see.  Our advantage, rather, was attitude: we had learned from=20
Ben Graham that the key to successful investing was the purchase=20
of shares in good businesses when market prices were at a large=20
discount from underlying business values.

     Most institutional investors in the early 1970s, on the=20
other hand, regarded business value as of only minor relevance=20
when they were deciding the prices at which they would buy or=20
sell.  This now seems hard to believe.  However, these=20
institutions were then under the spell of academics at=20
prestigious business schools who were preaching a newly-fashioned=20
theory: the stock market was totally efficient, and therefore=20
calculations of business value - and even thought, itself - were=20
of no importance in investment activities. (We are enormously=20
indebted to those academics: what could be more advantageous in=20
an intellectual contest - whether it be bridge, chess, or stock=20
selection than to have opponents who have been taught that=20
thinking is a waste of energy?)

     Through 1973 and 1974, WPC continued to do fine as a=20
business, and intrinsic value grew.  Nevertheless, by yearend=20
1974 our WPC holding showed a loss of about 25%, with market=20
value at $8 million against our cost of $10.6 million.  What we=20
had thought ridiculously cheap a year earlier had become a good=20
bit cheaper as the market, in its infinite wisdom, marked WPC=20
stock down to well below 20 cents on the dollar of intrinsic=20
value.

     You know the happy outcome.  Kay Graham, CEO of WPC, had the=20
brains and courage to repurchase large quantities of stock for=20
the company at those bargain prices, as well as the managerial=20
skills necessary to dramatically increase business values. =20
Meanwhile, investors began to recognize the exceptional economics=20
of the business and the stock price moved closer to underlying=20
value.  Thus, we experienced a triple dip: the company=92s business=20
value soared upward, per-share business value increased=20
considerably faster because of stock repurchases and, with a=20
narrowing of the discount, the stock price outpaced the gain in=20
per-share business value.

     We hold all of the WPC shares we bought in 1973, except for=20
those sold back to the company in 1985=92s proportionate=20
redemption.  Proceeds from the redemption plus yearend market=20
value of our holdings total $221 million.

     If we had invested our $10.6 million in any of a half-dozen=20
media companies that were investment favorites in mid-1973, the=20
value of our holdings at yearend would have been in the area of=20
$40 - $60 million.  Our gain would have far exceeded the gain in=20
the general market, an outcome reflecting the exceptional=20
economics of the media business.  The extra $160 million or so we=20
gained through ownership of WPC came, in very large part, from=20
the superior nature of the managerial decisions made by Kay as=20
compared to those made by managers of most media companies.  Her=20
stunning business success has in large part gone unreported but=20
among Berkshire shareholders it should not go unappreciated.

     Our Capital Cities purchase, described in the next section,=20
required me to leave the WPC Board early in 1986.  But we intend=20
to hold indefinitely whatever WPC stock FCC rules allow us to. =20
We expect WPC=92s business values to grow at a reasonable rate, and=20
we know that management is both able and shareholder-oriented. =20
However, the market now values the company at over $1.8 billion,=20
and there is no way that the value can progress from that level=20
at a rate anywhere close to the rate possible when the company=92s=20
valuation was only $100 million.  Because market prices have also=20
been bid up for our other holdings, we face the same vastly-
reduced potential throughout our portfolio.

     You will notice that we had a significant holding in=20
Beatrice Companies at yearend.  This is a short-term arbitrage=20
holding - in effect, a parking place for money (though not a=20
totally safe one, since deals sometimes fall through and create=20
