Capital Cities/ABC, Inc. (=93Cap Cities=94) at $172.50 per share, the=20
market price of such shares at the time the commitment was made=20
early in March, 1985.  I=92ve been on record for many years about=20
the management of Cap Cities: I think it is the best of any=20
publicly-owned company in the country.  And Tom Murphy and Dan=20
Burke are not only great managers, they are precisely the sort of=20
fellows that you would want your daughter to marry.  It is a=20
privilege to be associated with them - and also a lot of fun, as=20
any of you who know them will understand.

     Our purchase of stock helped Cap Cities finance the $3.5=20
billion acquisition of American Broadcasting Companies.  For Cap=20
Cities, ABC is a major undertaking whose economics are likely to=20
be unexciting over the next few years.  This bothers us not an=20
iota; we can be very patient. (No matter how great the talent or=20
effort, some things just take time: you can=92t produce a baby in=20
one month by getting nine women pregnant.)

     As evidence of our confidence, we have executed an unusual=20
agreement: for an extended period Tom, as CEO (or Dan, should he=20
be CEO) votes our stock.  This arrangement was initiated by=20
Charlie and me, not by Tom.  We also have restricted ourselves in=20
various ways regarding sale of our shares.  The object of these=20
restrictions is to make sure that our block does not get sold to=20
anyone who is a large holder (or intends to become a large=20
holder) without the approval of management, an arrangement=20
similar to ones we initiated some years ago at GEICO and=20
Washington Post.

     Since large blocks frequently command premium prices, some=20
might think we have injured Berkshire financially by creating=20
such restrictions.  Our view is just the opposite.  We feel the=20
long-term economic prospects for these businesses - and, thus,=20
for ourselves as owners - are enhanced by the arrangements.  With=20
them in place, the first-class managers with whom we have aligned=20
ourselves can focus their efforts entirely upon running the=20
businesses and maximizing long-term values for owners.  Certainly=20
this is much better than having those managers distracted by=20
=93revolving-door capitalists=94 hoping to put the company =93in play=94.=
=20
(Of course, some managers place their own interests above those=20
of the company and its owners and deserve to be shaken up - but,=20
in making investments, we try to steer clear of this type.)

     Today, corporate instability is an inevitable consequence of=20
widely-diffused ownership of voting stock.  At any time a major=20
holder can surface, usually mouthing reassuring rhetoric but=20
frequently harboring uncivil intentions.  By circumscribing our=20
blocks of stock as we often do, we intend to promote stability=20
where it otherwise might be lacking.  That kind of certainty,=20
combined with a good manager and a good business, provides=20
excellent soil for a rich financial harvest.  That=92s the economic=20
case for our arrangements.

     The human side is just as important.  We don=92t want managers=20
we like and admire - and who have welcomed a major financial=20
commitment by us - to ever lose any sleep wondering whether=20
surprises might occur because of our large ownership.  I have=20
told them there will be no surprises, and these agreements put=20
Berkshire=92s signature where my mouth is.  That signature also=20
means the managers have a corporate commitment and therefore need=20
not worry if my personal participation in Berkshire=92s affairs=20
ends prematurely (a term I define as any age short of three=20
digits).

     Our Cap Cities purchase was made at a full price, reflecting=20
the very considerable enthusiasm for both media stocks and media=20
properties that has developed in recent years (and that, in the=20
case of some property purchases, has approached a mania). it=92s no=20
field for bargains.  However, our Cap Cities investment allies us=20
with an exceptional combination of properties and people - and we=20
like the opportunity to participate in size.

     Of course, some of you probably wonder why we are now buying=20
