good earner.

     The company has sales of about $700 million derived from 17=20
businesses, many leaders in their fields.  Return on invested=20
capital is good to excellent for most of these businesses.  Some=20
well-known products are Kirby home-care systems, Campbell=20
Hausfeld air compressors, and Wayne burners and water pumps.

     World Book, Inc. - accounting for about 40% of Scott=20
Fetzer=92s sales and a bit more of its income - is by far the=20
company=92s largest operation.  It also is by far the leader in its=20
industry, selling more than twice as many encyclopedia sets=20
annually as its nearest competitor.  In fact, it sells more sets=20
in the U.S. than its four biggest competitors combined.

     Charlie and I have a particular interest in the World Book=20
operation because we regard its encyclopedia as something=20
special.  I=92ve been a fan (and user) for 25 years, and now have=20
grandchildren consulting the sets just as my children did.  World=20
Book is regularly rated the most useful encyclopedia by teachers,=20
librarians and consumer buying guides.  Yet it sells for less=20
than any of its major competitors. Childcraft, another World=20
Book, Inc. product, offers similar value.  This combination of=20
exceptional products and modest prices at World Book, Inc. helped=20
make us willing to pay the price demanded for Scott Fetzer,=20
despite declining results for many companies in the direct-
