     The Scott Fetzer acquisition, plus major growth in our=20
insurance business, should push revenues above $2 billion in=20
1986, more than double those of 1985.

<b>Miscellaneous</b>

     The Scott Fetzer purchase illustrates our somewhat haphazard=20
approach to acquisitions.  We have no master strategy, no=20
corporate planners delivering us insights about socioeconomic=20
trends, and no staff to investigate a multitude of ideas=20
presented by promoters and intermediaries.  Instead, we simply=20
hope that something sensible comes along - and, when it does, we=20
act.

     To give fate a helping hand, we again repeat our regular=20
=93business wanted=94 ad.  The only change from last year=92s copy is=20
in (1): because we continue to want any acquisition we make to=20
have a measurable impact on Berkshire=92s financial results, we=20
have raised our minimum profit requirement.

     Here=92s what we=92re looking for:
     (1) large purchases (at least $10 million of after-tax=20
         earnings),
     (2) demonstrated consistent earning power (future=20
         projections are of little interest to us, nor are=20
         =93turn-around=94 situations),
     (3) businesses earning good returns on equity while=20
         employing little or no debt,
     (4) management in place (we can=92t supply it),
     (5) simple businesses (if there=92s lots of technology, we=20
         won=92t understand it),
     (6) an offering price (we don=92t want to waste our time=20
         or that of the seller by talking, even preliminarily,=20
         about a transaction when price is unknown).
=20
     We will not engage in unfriendly takeovers.  We can promise=20
complete confidentiality and a very fast answer - customarily=20
within five minutes - as to whether we=92re interested.  We prefer=20
to buy for cash, but will consider issuance of stock when we=20
receive as much in intrinsic business value as we give.  Indeed,=20
following recent advances in the price of Berkshire stock,=20
transactions involving stock issuance may be quite feasible.  We=20
invite potential sellers to check us out by contacting people=20
with whom we have done business in the past.  For the right=20
business - and the right people - we can provide a good home.

     On the other hand, we frequently get approached about=20
acquisitions that don=92t come close to meeting our tests: new=20
ventures, turnarounds, auction-like sales, and the ever-popular=20
(among brokers) =93I=92m-sure-something-will-work-out-if-you-people-
get-to-know-each-other=94.  None of these attracts us in the least.

